  "cover_text": "The court system in Sheridan, Arkansas is a government body to conduct justice in the community. The judges stand behind the cases they invest their trust. Localized trial courts are bound by state law and seat-based area courts operate within significant geographical boundaries. Principles that direct how the judiciary operates are often embedded in statutes, resulting in a large, formalized Grand Judge System. Cases are divided into two courts, a criminal court and a civil court, headed by county court judges, from 11 jurisdictions/homes of the 18 county courts. County courts operate in the state. Each county court has a judge, two attorneys and two clerks. When a county court isn’t operating in its next largest justice area, it usually generates an assistant justice area court. This jurisdiction is more metropolitan and usually employs a dozen deputies.\nThe sheriff’s",
